---
import { BLIPFOTO_SUPPORT_REQUEST } from '../blipfoto-links';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import StatusDisclaimer from '../components/StatusDisclaimer.astro';

// Every statement about what the monitor does here describes packages/b-status as built
// (probe, retry, snapshot, shares). Re-check this page whenever that changes (b-oss#493).
const sections = [
  ['who', 'Who runs this'],
  ['what', 'What we check'],
  ['often', 'How often'],
  ['states', 'Up, Down and Unknown'],
  ['charts', 'Reading the charts'],
  ['shared', 'Shared problems'],
  ['uptime', 'Uptime figures'],
  ['times', 'Times and days'],
  ['privacy', 'Privacy'],
] as const;
---

<Layout
  title="How the Blipfoto status page works (unofficial) · b-oss"
  description="What the unofficial Blipfoto status page checks, how often, and how to read it."
  nav="status"
>
  <StatusDisclaimer slot="below-header" />

  <div class="status-page hw-page">
    <p class="hw-back"><a href="status.html">&lsaquo; Back to status</a></p>
    <header class="st-head">
      <p class="st-eyebrow">Status</p>
      <h1>How this works</h1>
      <p class="st-sub-lead">What we check, how often, and what the page is telling you.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="hw-layout">
      <nav class="hw-toc" aria-label="On this page">
        <p class="st-eyebrow">On this page</p>
        <ul>
          {sections.map(([id, label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>)}
        </ul>
      </nav>

      <div class="hw-body">
        <section id="who" aria-labelledby="hw-who">
          <h2 id="hw-who">Who runs this</h2>
          <p>
            This page is run by b-oss, a small open-source project that makes tools for Blipfoto users.
            It isn’t run by Blipfoto.
          </p>
          <p>
            That means we can tell you whether something looks wrong, but we can’t fix it. If Blipfoto
            isn’t working for you, Blipfoto Support is the only way to get it looked at.
          </p>
          <p><a class="st-btn st-btn-primary" href={BLIPFOTO_SUPPORT_REQUEST} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Contact Blipfoto Support ↗</a></p>
        </section>

        <section id="what" aria-labelledby="hw-what">
          <h2 id="hw-what">What we check</h2>
          <p>We check two parts of Blipfoto separately, because they can fail separately.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Site</strong> is blipfoto.com in a web browser. We load the browse page, then one
              of the entries on it, the way a browser would.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>API</strong> is what apps such as b-ark and b-mobile use to talk to Blipfoto. We
              make the same kind of simple, read-only requests those apps make, using an app registration
              created just for this monitor, and check that we get a proper answer rather than an error.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            We also check that the entry’s photo loads. Some things affect both parts: if photos aren’t
            loading, the Site and the API both show as down, even though it’s one problem.
          </p>
          <p>
            If a check fails we try again a few seconds later, and only a failure both times counts as
            down. A check also counts as failed if it comes back, but far too slowly to be usable.
          </p>
          <p>
            All our checks run from one place, outside Blipfoto’s own network. If a problem is only in
            some places, or only with your own connection, we may not see it. We don’t log in, and we
            only read public pages.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="often" aria-labelledby="hw-often">
          <h2 id="hw-often">How often</h2>
          <p>
            We check every 15 minutes. The status at the top of the page shows how long ago the last check
            was, and the page tells you when the next one is due. It doesn’t refresh itself, so nothing
            moves while you’re reading.
          </p>
          <p>Because we only look every 15 minutes:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>a problem that lasts less than 15 minutes might not show up at all;</li>
            <li>start times, end times and durations are approximate, to the nearest 15 minutes.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="states" aria-labelledby="hw-states">
          <h2 id="hw-states">Up, Down and Unknown</h2>
          <dl class="hw-states">
            <dt><span class="st-badge st-badge-up"><span aria-hidden="true">✓</span> Up</span></dt>
            <dd>Our last check worked.</dd>
            <dt><span class="st-badge st-badge-down"><span aria-hidden="true">✕</span> Down</span></dt>
            <dd>Our last check failed, and so did the retry: the page or request didn’t load, was far too slow, or came back with an error.</dd>
            <dt><span class="st-badge st-badge-unknown"><span aria-hidden="true">?</span> Unknown</span></dt>
            <dd>
              We haven’t been able to check recently. That’s a problem on our side, not necessarily
              Blipfoto’s, and we don’t count it as up or down.
            </dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <section id="charts" aria-labelledby="hw-charts">
          <h2 id="hw-charts">Reading the charts</h2>
          <p>The charts only show problems. An empty lane is good news: every check in that period worked.</p>
          <ul class="hw-key">
            <li><span class="st-key st-key-down" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><strong>Red block or bar:</strong> our checks found a problem. In History, the taller the bar, the longer it was down that day.</span></li>
            <li><span class="st-key st-key-nodata" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><strong>Striped grey:</strong> no data. We couldn’t check at that time.</span></li>
            <li><span class="st-key st-key-shared" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><strong>Amber bars:</strong> people shared a problem. See below.</span></li>
            <li><span><strong>A bar with a white cut</strong> was down for more than 4 hours. We cut very long bars short so that short problems stay visible; the real time is shown above the bar.</span></li>
          </ul>
          <p>Select any mark to see the details. On a phone, use the Earlier and Later buttons under each chart.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="shared" aria-labelledby="hw-shared">
          <h2 id="hw-shared">Shared problems</h2>
          <p>
            Anyone can share a problem they’re having from the status page. Shared problems are counted
            separately for the website and for apps, and show as amber bars under each chart.
          </p>
          <p>
            They are not verified, and they never change the uptime figures. Sometimes people share a
            problem when our checks found nothing. That can happen when the problem is with one
            feature, such as uploads, or affects some people but not the place we check from. It’s
            still useful to know.
          </p>
          <div class="hw-callout" role="note">
            <strong>Sharing a problem here doesn’t tell Blipfoto.</strong> If you’d like it fixed,
            please also <a href={BLIPFOTO_SUPPORT_REQUEST}>contact Blipfoto Support</a>.
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="uptime" aria-labelledby="hw-uptime">
          <h2 id="hw-uptime">Uptime figures</h2>
          <p>
            Uptime is the share of the time we checked that each part was working. Times when we couldn’t
            check are left out, so they don’t count for or against.
          </p>
          <p>
            We always round down. 100% means we found no problems at all; very high figures show extra
            decimal places (such as 99.999%) so they are never rounded up to 100%.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="times" aria-labelledby="hw-times">
          <h2 id="hw-times">Times and days</h2>
          <p>Times of day on the status page are UK time.</p>
          <p>
            The History chart and the uptime figures count each day from midnight to midnight UTC, the
            same as GMT. In summer that’s an hour behind UK clocks, so a problem just after midnight UK
            time can appear on the previous day.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="privacy" aria-labelledby="hw-privacy">
          <h2 id="hw-privacy">Privacy</h2>
          <p>
            You don’t need an account to share a problem. We keep your answers and the time you shared
            them, and nothing else about you.
          </p>
          <p>
            So that the counts stay fair, the number of shares from one place is limited. To do that we
            keep an anonymous marker for about half an hour, then it is deleted. It is a one-way
            scrambled form of your connection’s address, not the address itself.
          </p>
          <p>
            Sharing uses Cloudflare’s Turnstile anti-bot check. The code for this page and the monitor
            is open source: <a href="https://github.com/IanMStevenson/b-oss">see the source code</a>.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
